;; Docs for std/atomic.bjo, read through (:doc #:file "atomic.bjodoc"). The
;; texts are samizdat markup: code in prose is @code{...}.

(:doc #:module
  (summary "Atomics: a cell that threads can read and write without a lock, with integer operations for int, uint, long and ulong.")
  (see make-atomic atomic-update! atomic-compare-and-swap! AtomicInteger)
  (example """
    (import (std atomic))

    (def hits (make-atomic 0L))
    (atomic-increment! hits)                 ; 1L
    (atomic-add! hits 10L)                   ; 11L
    (atomic-swap! hits 0L)                   ; 11L, and hits is now 0L

    (def names (make-atomic (list "ada")))
    (atomic-update! names (fun (ns) (Cons "bea" ns)))   ; ("bea" "ada")
    """)
  (reference """
    An @code{(Atomic T)} holds one value of type T. Every operation on it is
    atomic: two threads that use the same atomic at the same time see the
    operations one after the other, never mixed.

    @strong{What the operations return.} @code{atomic-swap!} and
    @code{atomic-compare-and-swap!} return the value from before the call.
    @code{atomic-update!}, @code{atomic-increment!},
    @code{atomic-decrement!}, @code{atomic-add!} and @code{atomic-sub!}
    return the new value.

    @strong{Equality.} @code{atomic-compare-and-swap!} compares the value in
    the atomic with the expected value. A reference type, such as a record,
    a list or a string, compares by identity, as @code{eq?} does: an equal
    value that is a different object does not match. A value type, such as a
    struct, an @code{Option} or one of the four integers, compares by value.

    @strong{Only the stored value is atomic.} If the atomic holds a mutable
    object, such as an array or a mutable vec, a change made inside that
    object is not protected. Store a new value instead, for example with
    @code{atomic-update!}.

    @strong{Integers.} The trait @code{AtomicInteger} gives
    @code{atomic-increment!}, @code{atomic-decrement!}, @code{atomic-add!}
    and @code{atomic-sub!}. Bjolang implements it for @code{int},
    @code{uint}, @code{long} and @code{ulong}, with
    @code{System.Threading.Interlocked}. These wrap on overflow: the int
    maximum plus 1 is the int minimum, and 0u minus 1u is the uint maximum.
    On any other type the integer operations are a compile error.

    @strong{The width of an integer} comes from type inference, as it does
    for any number. @code{(make-atomic 0)} is an @code{(Atomic int)}. Write
    @code{(make-atomic 0L)} or @code{(make-atomic 0UL)} for a long or a ulong
    counter, or give the type: @code{(: hits (Atomic ulong))}. A counter of a
    type that is too narrow wraps.
    """))

(:doc Atomic
  (summary "A cell that holds one value of type %a, with atomic operations.")
  (tparam %a "The type of the value.")
  (see make-atomic atomic-ref AtomicInteger))

(:doc make-atomic
  (summary "A new atomic that holds value.")
  (arg value "The first value.")
  (returns "A new atomic.")
  (example """
    (make-atomic 0)            ; an (Atomic int)
    (make-atomic 0L)           ; an (Atomic long)
    (make-atomic 0UL)          ; an (Atomic ulong)
    (make-atomic (list 1 2))   ; an (Atomic (List int))
    """)
  (reference """
    The type of the atomic is the type of @code{value}, from normal type
    inference. A number without a suffix is an @code{int} unless something
    else says. A counter of a type that is too narrow wraps on overflow, so
    write @code{0L} or @code{0UL}, or annotate the type, for a counter that
    can pass the int maximum.
    """))

(:doc atomic-ref
  (summary "The value in the atomic.")
  (arg a "The atomic.")
  (returns "The value that the atomic holds now."))

(:doc atomic-set!
  (summary "Stores value in the atomic.")
  (arg a "The atomic.")
  (arg value "The new value.")
  (reference """
    The store is a full fence. Reads and writes after it in the same thread
    do not move before it.
    """))

(:doc atomic-swap!
  (summary "Stores value in the atomic, and returns the value from before.")
  (arg a "The atomic.")
  (arg value "The new value.")
  (returns "The value that the atomic held before the call."))

(:doc atomic-compare-and-swap!
  (summary "Stores desired if the atomic holds expected, and returns the value from before.")
  (arg a "The atomic.")
  (arg expected "The value that the atomic must hold for the store to happen.")
  (arg desired "The value to store.")
  (returns "The value that the atomic held before the call. The store happened if and only if this value equals expected.")
  (example """
    (def a (make-atomic 5))
    (atomic-compare-and-swap! a 5 6)   ; 5, and a holds 6
    (atomic-compare-and-swap! a 5 7)   ; 6, and a still holds 6
    """)
  (reference """
    A reference type compares by identity, as @code{eq?} does. A value type
    compares by value. So a record that is equal to the stored one, but is a
    different object, does not match.
    """))

(:doc atomic-update!
  (summary "Stores f of the value in the atomic, and returns the new value.")
  (arg a "The atomic.")
  (arg f "Makes the new value from the old value.")
  (returns "The value that was stored.")
  (example """
    (def seen (make-atomic (list)))
    (atomic-update! seen (fun (xs) (Cons 3 xs)))   ; (3)
    """)
  (reference """
    If another thread stores a value between the read and the store, the
    update tries again with the new value. So under contention f can run
    more than once. f must have no side effects: no printing, no I/O, and no
    change to other state.
    """))

(:doc AtomicInteger
  (summary "The integer operations of an atomic: increment, decrement, add and subtract.")
  (tparam %a "The integer type in the atomic.")
  (see atomic-increment! atomic-add! atomic-update!)
  (reference """
    Bjolang implements this trait for @code{int}, @code{uint}, @code{long}
    and @code{ulong}, with @code{System.Threading.Interlocked}. At those
    types a call is a direct call to @code{Interlocked}. A call on an atomic
    of any other type is a compile error.

    The trait is open, so a program can implement it for a type of its own.
    That implementation does not get the @code{Interlocked} path: write its
    methods with @code{atomic-update!}.

    Every implementation must follow these laws:

    @itemlist{
      @item{Increment, decrement, add and subtract are atomic.}
      @item{They return the new value.}
      @item{The built-in implementations wrap on overflow, as two's
        complement does. An implementation of your own must document what
        it does on overflow.}
    }
    """)
  (example """
    (type (: Score (Struct (: points int))))

    (impl (AtomicInteger Score)
      (defun (atomic-increment! a) (atomic-add! a (Score (points 1))))
      (defun (atomic-decrement! a) (atomic-sub! a (Score (points 1))))
      (defun (atomic-add! a n)
        (atomic-update! a (fun (s) (Score (points (+ (.points s) (.points n)))))))
      (defun (atomic-sub! a n)
        (atomic-update! a (fun (s) (Score (points (- (.points s) (.points n))))))))
    """))

(:doc atomic-increment!
  (summary "Adds 1 to the integer in the atomic, and returns the new value.")
  (arg a "The atomic.")
  (returns "The new value. The built-in integers wrap on overflow."))

(:doc atomic-decrement!
  (summary "Subtracts 1 from the integer in the atomic, and returns the new value.")
  (arg a "The atomic.")
  (returns "The new value. The built-in integers wrap on overflow."))

(:doc atomic-add!
  (summary "Adds n to the integer in the atomic, and returns the new value.")
  (arg a "The atomic.")
  (arg n "The number to add.")
  (returns "The new value. The built-in integers wrap on overflow."))

(:doc atomic-sub!
  (summary "Subtracts n from the integer in the atomic, and returns the new value.")
  (arg a "The atomic.")
  (arg n "The number to subtract.")
  (returns "The new value. The built-in integers wrap on overflow."))
